 ## Overview
-DVR-MIP Interpolation is a dynamic visualization technique that transitions between Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) and Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) based on user interaction semantics [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of revealing inner data structures (such as vascular networks) that are occluded in standard DVR by gradually shifting the visual representation towards MIP during interactions like rotation or zooming [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike static transfer functions, this method uses a continuous interpolation parameter to dynamically adjust the rendering style, allowing users to explore focus+context relationships in real-time [S2, unofficial].
+DVR-MIP Interpolation is a dynamic visualization technique that transitions between Direct Volume Rendering (DVR) and Maximum Intensity Projection (MIP) based on user interaction semantics [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of revealing inner data structures (such as vascular networks) that are occluded in standard DVR by gradually shifting the visual representation towards MIP during interactions like rotation or zooming [S1, unofficial]. Unlike static transfer functions, this method uses a continuous interpolation parameter to dynamically adjust the rendering style, allowing users to explore focus+context relationships in real-time [S2, unofficial].
 
